got a 74 reduction combined for  these two tests and the total amount of  utilization and a big thank you  um out to the clinical team for really  supporting us and doing this initiative  okay so that was the really easy one the  second one  was trying to get rid of the ck for  cardiac injury  this was a much more than it was partly  a systems problem  but a lot of the doctors particularly  are cardiologists and some of our  cardiac surgeons and  cardiovascular intensivists still loved  this test  and so this created a little bit more  work fortunately i had  dr raza who at the time was a general  internal medicine resident who wanted to  do general internal medicine  um and he worked on this project but by  the time he finished i'd converted him  into understanding that hematology was  the best sub-specialty and  in july of next year he will be one of  our hematology  residents  so um we've gotten to this dotted line  at tiny work so originally uh we had  done a ck and a ckmb  for cardiac injury and then troponin  came along and we added the troponin  without taking the other two away  we eventually were able to just stop  offering the ckmb test but they still  kept ordering the ck but we never went  to the final distance  and went straight to just a troponin  only  and so that was our challenge for this  quality improvement project  so the first thing like we did with the  first project is we looked at the data  so this is just for one month in  september and we looked at you know how  many doctors were ordering  um uh uh ck or troponin and what the  ratio was and how often they were  ordered together  so our ck to troponin ratio was running  at about 88  it was super high we didn't really have  a clue of where we needed to get this to  um but we wanted to get it below 25  um and 78 of the time these two tests  were ordered together so we were pretty  certain that they were being ordered for  cardiac injury when you really  shouldn't be and almost all the tests or