 single patient  rather than picking and choosing which  patients needed this so  here i kind of hit this roadblock  but unfortunately i had been in norway  and i was giving a debate  and i was giving a debate about one  blood product over another with the  u.s military which i'm happy to say i  won  but during that i had this great idea  and i thought ah  i'm gonna challenge them to a debate so  i  emailed my chief of cardiology and i  said well let's do a debate since we  can't get a final resolution  we're gonna do a pro ck versus con ck  for myocardial injury  i'll do the con you need to get somebody  on your team to step up  the plate to debate me and 48 hours  later by the time i got back from norway  the papers were signed and they were on  my desk and we were able to pull the ck  from all their order sets  so i never ever actually had to do this  debate  and so in this quality improvement  project we did almost the same thing uh  once we got  the approval to move forward and get  them convinced of the need to do this  we obviously analyzed the data we had  wide stakeholder consultation  we removed the ck from every order group  in the computer system  every order set and then we did ongoing  monthly analysis  so we knew oh you know the cardiac word  they're still ordering it why's that  and then you know we found on our  cardiac  team that the clerks just had a little  cue card which told them when this kind  of patient was admitted these are the  tests they did and the ck was still on  that  even though we ordered it from the um  the computerized order sets  and it worked um we're now sitting at  about a third of the time they order  troponin we get a ck  we still have room for improvement and  we're still working on this but we've  made a major dent  in the ordering of ck  and one of the ways i really like to  look at these grouped together tests  that you're trying to  ungroup let's start with one of these  venn diagrams you can see there before  there was almost complete overlap  between the troponin and the ck  amount and so in the post intervention  okay i shrunk the ck they're ordering  less of them  but also less coupling of them so i  think it's a really good way  in laboratory medicine to describe how  we're using these two tests  okay we're just waiting for our code  white to finish here  so the so the third test also had a  completely  uh third project had a completely  different  um problem  so um we uh recruited  megan mclavish who wanted to do a  quality improvement project she was  another  second year internal medicine resident  one of the good things is the internal  medicine residents have to do quality  improvement projects during their  training  so paul yep and i who's our division  lead for clinical chemistry and i  have been working with her to try and  figure this out  and we actually harness the middleware  to put controls on repeats not just for  the b12 that i'm going to show you here  but simultaneously  for the hemoglobin a1c and the vitamin d  and so we were able to use the same  technique  to control multiple paths  and most of the problem that we found  was that  hospitalized patients were getting  duplicate tests for b12 because the  doctor couldn't see in the laboratory  system  that they'd already had that test two  days ago and the test result was normal  because they couldn't see it they  figured it had never been done and so  they  they ordered it again  and so when we looked at the b12  utilization  baseline we were running about 6000  tests per year  of which about eight percent were  happening in the emergency department  38 percent in their in the inpatient  locations and  about half in the outpatient locations  in the  outpatient locations it took a long time  more than six months before somebody had  a repeat test  they were doing a much better job but on  the  inpatient side the median time to your  next test was about 49 to 89 days  so it was really soon after their  previous test  and so we calculated that by putting  controls where you couldn't have a  repeat test  after a previously normal test within 12  weeks  and so uh we estimated that would that  would yield us about 8.6  improvement in our performance  and so um very similar to other projects  first we gathered information and data  and had meetings with people that were  ordering these three tests  we went to the medical advisory  committee and we got them to approve  us putting control so they couldn't have  a repeat test and so it was all done  above board it wasn't something sneaky  we did in the background  and then uh paul yep worked tirelessly  for several months  to put in all the logic in the  middleware  to make this happen and do an extensive  validation  so we didn't mess up when we went live  and then we implemented the rules in  early 2020  and so just for an example you know they  paul had to put  17 different conditions on this so you  know for example  the computer had to look and say okay  was there a previous result was it  within the last 80 days  instead of doing 90 days we gave them a  little bit of wiggle room and we said 80  days  was the test result normal and where for  normal we hit  we just you know put that at 250 which  most of us would agree is okay  we looked at the ordering location  making sure it's not being ordered by  research  if for some research um program they  need to b12 done on patients for say in  the neurology research program  they're paying for it they can still  have it and look for previous error  flags maybe the previous result wasn't  um valid and so they're reordering it to  try and get a valid result  and so there were all these different  conditions so we made sure that we  canceled the right test but didn't  cancel tests that actually need to be  done  um and then the physician in their  electronic inbox  if they ordered a test within those 80  days they get a nice little message this  test was not performed due to previous  order  within the preceding three months but  you can contact the laboratory within  five days we still have the tube and  we'll run that test for you  i'm happy to report that basically  nobody called the lab asking us to  run these tests and so uh people were  you know  okay to go back and look and see oh  there was a test i can go look at a  previous result  and then a in addition to going to the  medical advisory committee  um uh a  email message went out to all laboratory  users all the physicians to say  hey we just put controls on the b12 we  also did the controls on the vitamin d  at the same time  also allowing no repeat tests within a  certain interval  explaining why we did this what the  evidence for this  is um and as far as i know  nobody uh complained and so doctors are  really  generally buying into the fact that we  have to use our healthcare resources  more effectively otherwise you know it's  just going to cost  so much money that lots of patients  aren't going to get the care that they  need  and here's the results so this went live  as i said in january  of uh uh 2020 and you can see there  the utilization went down it went down  for a very good reason and that would be  covis  um because lots of patients don't want  to have any laboratory testing done  but you can see also there in that red  color are all the cancel tests  so where we estimated that around eight  percent um  of tests would get uh cancel we're  pretty bang on at seven point five  percent so despite covid  we're pretty bang on on what we were  going to be able to achieve with these  middleware controls  and you can see here on the  pre-intervention to the post  intervention and i'll remind you i  didn't have to do any of this analysis i  got my statistician to do it  if i had done this analysis it would  have taken me a week whereas  she could have done it in a day so you  can see there  of people that went on to have repeats  um having a repeat in the in within  three months was about 50  of the repeats um versus we dropped that  number to 9.5  having a repeat within that time period  uh so  a very effective intervention  you can also see that the median time to  re a repeat  went up dramatically  and we also as a balancing measure every  time you do a quality improvement  project  you want to think about okay what are  the downsides you know how can i cause  harm by my intervention  and so two of the things that we wanted  to look at was how many times  did we run the test when we shouldn't  you know shouldn't run the test and that  happened about 20 times it's not so bad  but we had three tests that should have  been run but that weren't run  that we're still trying to figure out  why did the computer not run them  because there was no good reason  so we're still monitoring this but  really  crucial that you monitor this the 20  that were run  um that shouldn't have been run they  were really good reasons for this  so we don't run a b12 on a saturday or  sunday  so if a doctor orders a b12 on a  saturday and then they say oh my  goodness there was no b12 result  ordered again on sunday well it still  won't get run on the sunday  they'll both be put on the analyzer to  be run on the monday  um but uh because they're being run  simultaneously there is no valid result  and both of them will come across and be  run  at the same time so as you build your  middleware rules for this really think  really carefully  of all the possible permutations and  combinations  that could go wrong uh so that this  doesn't happen um  in addition monitor for it uh so you can  sort of slowly tweak your rules  until they're just perfect  okay so now i'd like to just you know  review those objectives that we had  um and really summarize what i've talked  about  so the first thing is to identify areas  of inefficiency in your lab that need  improving  so really improv important to look at  your data  talk to your clinicians find out what  they think is an o