 to  take questions  and comments criticism thank you  and thank you dr callum for that  informative presentation  we will now start the q a portion of the  webinar if you have any questions you'd  like to ask please do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  so let's get started we already have  some great  questions coming in  our first question dr kellum is what  laboratory inefficiency  issues and problems do you find most of  and what problems would you say are most  important  and a priority to solve first  yeah so i don't think there's a one good  answer  for everybody and every hospital is  going to have a different  setup so i can tell you  you know when i think about my lab now  in terms of where we've gotten to  um we sort of fix all the low-hanging  fruit and my one of my biggest  um pushes right now for me is to get rid  of the paper  because to me the paper is inefficient  it creates extra workload for the  clinicians  um and so we're working on that but  that's because i'm at a different point  i've already fixed some of the other  things  so i think you need to look at your own  statistics you got to look at your own  processes  and figure out what's your top thing  don't try and start  14 different projects at the same time  just start one  two get them done reevaluate them and  move on to the next project  thank you so much that sounds like good  advice for everybody  our next question does the choosing  wisely initiative  specific guidelines and recommendations  to reduce  unnecessary expenses related to the  reduction in length of  hospital stay and also inappropriate  utilization of  high cost medications  yeah so if you look at the choosing  wisely  recommendations um they don't have a lot  of verbiage there  but there is quite a concise paragraph  on  say you know  say for blood transfusion you should not  be ordering more than one red cell at a  time  and it gives a justification as well as  some